│ patch_name               │ softmin_gradient_heilbronn_optimizer                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the random point generation with a deterministic multi-restart projected gradient │
│                          │ ascent optimizer. The objective is a smooth soft-min (LogSumExp) approximation of the     │
│                          │ smallest triangle area over all 286 triangles, with a sharpening (annealing) parameter p  │
│                          │ that increases over iterations so the soft-min converges to the true minimum. Gradients   │
│                          │ are computed by vectorized central finite differences over all 26 coordinates in a single │
│                          │ batched area evaluation (52 perturbed configurations at once), which is very cheap.       │
│                          │ Points are projected back onto the unit square each step (the problem is scale-invariant, │
│                          │ and fixing the containing region makes maximizing raw min-area equivalent to maximizing   │
│                          │ the normalized ratio up to hull shape). Multiple restarts (random plus structured seeds   │
│                          │ like the regular 13-gon and 12-gon+center, which exploit the known 3-fold/rotational      │
│                          │ symmetry heuristics) are evaluated with the true metric (smallest triangle area / convex  │
│                          │ hull area), and the best configuration is returned. A fixed seed guarantees               │
│                          │ reproducibility. This should raise the normalized minimum triangle area from ~0.00 to the │
│                          │ ~0.02-0.03 range, pushing combined_score near or above 1.0.                               │
