│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multistart_hardmin_optimizer                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ Two key issues limit the current solution: (1) the soft-min gradient spreads the update   │
│                          │ over all triangles weighted by proximity to the minimum, diluting the effort on the       │
│                          │ actual bottleneck triangles, and (2) a single initialization risks a poor local optimum.  │
│                          │ Heilbronn-type max-min problems are highly multi-modal, so a multi-start strategy over    │
│                          │ several ring geometries and seeds is the standard remedy. The new optimizer uses a        │
│                          │ *hard-min* vertex-repulsion scheme: at each iteration only the ~8 smallest triangles are  │
│                          │ enlarged by pushing their vertices along the gradient of their (signed) area, with an     │
│                          │ adaptive backtracking step (accept a move only if the new min does not decrease,          │
│                          │ otherwise shrink the step). This monotonically improves the true objective, avoids the    │
│                          │ ill-conditioned softmax, and converges robustly. Determinism is preserved via fixed       │
│                          │ seeds, and the best configuration across all restarts is returned.                        │
