│ patch_name               │ threefold_symmetry_hillclimb                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of optimizing all 26 free coordinates with soft-min gradients (which gets stuck   │
│                          │ in poor local minima from random starts), this approach exploits the strong empirical     │
│                          │ evidence that optimal Heilbronn configurations for n=13 exhibit 3-fold rotational         │
│                          │ symmetry. The configuration is parametrized as: one point at the center (the only fixed   │
│                          │ point of a 120° rotation) plus 4 "orbits" of 3 points each generated by rotating 4 base   │
│                          │ points by 0°, 120°, 240°. This reduces the search from 26 dimensions to just 8, making    │
│                          │ coordinate-descent hill climbing (with adaptive shrinking steps) extremely effective and  │
│                          │ fast. A final short symmetry-breaking polish on all 26 coordinates squeezes out extra     │
│                          │ area. The tiny search space means many deterministic seeds can be exhausted in well under │
│                          │ a second, reliably beating the benchmark score of ~0.0309.                                │
