│ patch_name               │ fast_basin_hopping_heilbronn13                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ All prior attempts score ~0.18 (min_area_normalized ≈ 0.006), far below the ~0.031        │
│                          │ benchmark. They rely on slow softmin gradient ascent that gets stuck in symmetric ring    │
│                          │ configurations where small triangles persist. This rewrite uses a completely different    │
│                          │ paradigm: fast vectorized evaluation of the exact objective (min triangle area / hull     │
│                          │ area) plus basin-hopping stochastic hill climbing. Each move perturbs one point; the move │
│                          │ is accepted only if the true (non-smoothed) objective improves. Adaptive step sizes plus  │
│                          │ repeated perturbation-restart (basin hopping) from multiple deterministic seeds escape    │
│                          │ the poor symmetric local optima that plague gradient methods. Since the exact objective   │
│                          │ is evaluated directly (not a soft approximation), progress is never lost to smoothing     │
│                          │ bias. The convex hull of the final configuration is rescaled to unit area, which only     │
│                          │ helps (interior points stay inside, hull area becomes exactly 1).                         │
