                        Patch Metadata - Gen 50/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ staged_funnel_rerank_and_richer_inits                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ Two improvements based on the Heilbronn problem structure and prior-gen lessons:          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Two-pass ranking funnel**: The coarse climb is cheap but its output ranking is       │
│                          │ noisy. Following the recommendation (Gen 34's lesson: surrogate and exact rankings        │
│                          │ disagree near plateaus), after climbing every candidate we run a short exact mini-climb   │
│                          │ to re-rank, then spend expensive basin-hopping polish only on the top-2 candidates. This  │
│                          │ avoids wasting polish budget on mediocre basins while adding a cheap second-round         │
│                          │ screening pass over all candidates.                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Richer initialization set**: Add two structured, high-quality initializations known  │
│                          │ to be good for Heilbronn-type point packing: (a) a hexagonal/triangular lattice patch     │
│                          │ (hexagonal close-packing analogue, which gives uniform Voronoi cells and avoids           │
│                          │ near-collinear triples), and (b) a 6-fold rotationally symmetric configuration (center +  │
│                          │ two hexagonal orbits) which guarantees large minimum triangles between orbit points.      │
│                          │ These give the climber better basins than the 13-gon (which has many nearly-collinear     │
│                          │ consecutive triples and is a known local-optimum trap).                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ These changes increase the chance of finding a better basin (higher min_area_normalized)  │
│                          │ at modest time cost, since the polish budget is concentrated rather than spread over 16   │
