│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ fast_hull_incremental_solver                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ Structural redesign around two bottlenecks identified from prior generations. (1) The     │
│                          │ convex hull area — the ratio denominator evaluated at every hill-climb step — was         │
│                          │ computed by a pure-Python monotone chain with Python-level loops; it is replaced by       │
│                          │ `scipy.spatial.ConvexHull` (qhull, C speed) with a vectorized numpy fallback. (2) The     │
│                          │ solver is reorganized into a `Solver` class encapsulating an incremental state (cached    │
│                          │ 286-triangle area array + cached hull area): moving point i touches only its ~66          │
│                          │ triangles, and the hull area is recomputed only when the moved point is (or becomes) a    │
│                          │ hull vertex; otherwise the cached denominator is reused. These savings are reinvested in  │
│                          │ more hill-climb and basin-hopping iterations inside the same wall-clock budget,           │
│                          │ converting speed directly into solution quality while keeping full determinism (fixed     │
│                          │ seeds, fixed iteration counts, identical input/output contract).                          │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
