│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ affine_anneal_hybrid                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of symmetry-constrained pattern search, this uses a fundamentally different       │
│                          │ paradigm: time-budgeted simulated annealing over all 26 coordinates with three move       │
│                          │ classes — (1) single-point perturbations, (2) small random affine (linear)                │
│                          │ transformations of the whole configuration (stretch/shear — which genuinely change the    │
│                          │ min-triangle/hull-area ratio and can escape local optima unreachable by single-point      │
│                          │ moves), and (3) global scaling about the centroid. All moves are accepted/rejected on the │
│                          │ exact true score (min triangle area / hull area). After each annealing run, a short       │
│                          │ greedy coordinate polish converges to a sharp local optimum. Multiple deterministic seeds │
│                          │ (3-fold symmetric, lattice, and random) are annealed within a fixed wall-clock budget,    │
│                          │ and the best configuration across all runs is returned. Annealing with affine moves       │
│                          │ explores the ratio landscape far more broadly than hill-climbing on a 4-point symmetric   │
│                          │ parametrization, which previously got stuck in symmetric basins.                          │
